\n\n2. **Conditions for Acute Triangle**:\n   - If \\( x \\) is the longest side (i.e., \\( x > 3 \\)), then:\n     \\[\n     2^2 + 3^2 > x^2 \\implies 4 + 9 > x^2 \\implies 13 > x^2 \\implies x < \\sqrt{13}\n     \\]\n     Combining with the triangle inequality, we get \\( 3 < x < \\sqrt{13} \\).\n\n   - If 3 is the longest side (i.e., \\( x \\leq 3 \\)), then:\n     \\[\n     2^2 + x^2 > 3^2 \\implies 4 + x^2 > 9 \\implies x^2 > 5 \\implies x > \\sqrt{5}\n     \\]\n     Combining with the triangle inequality, we get \\( \\sqrt{5} < x \\leq 3 \\).\n\n3. **Combining Both Cases**:\n   - For \\( x \\) to be the longest side: \\( 3 < x < \\sqrt{13} \\)\n   - For 3 to be the longest side: \\( \\sqrt{5} < x \\leq 3 \\)\n\n   Combining these intervals, the range of \\( x \\) is \\( \\sqrt{5} < x < \\sqrt{13} \\).\n\nThus, the correct answer is \\(\\boxed{B}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nBy the Pythagorean Theorem for acute triangles, we know that the square of any one side must be less than the sum of the squares of the other two sides. Given a triangle with sides $2$, $3$, and $x$, the conditions we need to satisfy are:\n\n1. The sum of the squares of the two smaller sides must be greater than the square of the longest side.\n2. The sum of the squares of the two smaller sides must be greater than the square of the longest side.\n3. The sum of the squares of the two sides must be greater than the square of the unknown side.\n\nLet us establish the inequalities:\n\n1. $2^2 + 3^2 > x^2$\n\n\\[ 4 + 9 > x^2 \\]\n\\[ 13 > x^2 \\]\n\\[ x < \\sqrt{13} \\]\n\n2. $2^2 + x^2 > 3^2$\n\n\\[ 4 + x^2 > 9 \\]\n\\[ x^2 > 5 \\]\n\\[ x > \\sqrt{5} \\]\n\n3. $3^2 + x^2 > 2^2$\n\n\\[ 9 + x^2 > 4 \\]\n\\[ x^2 > -5 \\]\n\nSince $x^2 > -5$ is always true for any real $x$, we respect the constraints from the other inequalities.\n\nCombining the valid inequalities from steps 1 and 2, we get:\n\n\\[ \\sqrt{5} < x < \\sqrt{13} \\]\n\nThus, the range of possible values for the third side \\( x \