nator:\n\n(x + 1)/x * sqrt(x + 1)/sqrt(x) = (1 + 1/x) * sqrt(1 + 1/x).\n\nBut maybe that's not any simpler. So perhaps the most compact form is - (2/3) (1 + 1/x)^(3/2) + C, or expressed in terms of x and (x + 1). Let me check if this answer differentiates back to the original integrand.\n\nLet's verify the result by differentiating:\n\nd/dx [ - (2/3)(1 + 1/x)^(3/2) + C ] = - (2/3) * (3/2) (1 + 1/x)^(1/2) * (-1/x\u00b2) + 0.\n\nSimplify:\n\n- (2/3)*(3/2) = -1, and the derivative becomes -1 * (1 + 1/x)^(1/2) * (-1/x\u00b2) = (1 + 1/x)^(1/2)/x\u00b2.\n\nBut earlier, we had the integrand as sqrt(1 + 1/x)/x\u00b2. Which matches. Therefore, the derivative is indeed the integrand. Therefore, the answer is correct.\n\nBut let's just cross-verify once more. Let me compute the derivative step by step.\n\nLet y = - (2/3)(1 + 1/x)^(3/2). Then dy/dx = - (2/3) * (3/2) * (1 + 1/x)^(1/2) * d/dx(1 + 1/x).\n\nThe derivative of 1 + 1/x is -1/x\u00b2. So:\n\ndy/dx = - (2/3)*(3/2)*(1 + 1/x)^(1/2)*(-1/x\u00b2) = (-1)*(1 + 1/x)^(1/2)*(-1/x\u00b2) = (1 + 1/x)^(1/2)/x\u00b2.\n\nWhich is exactly the integrand. So that's correct.\n\nBut let's also make sure that the substitution steps were correctly followed. The original integrand was sqrt(1 + x)/x^(5/2). After substitution, we ended up with sqrt(1 + 1/x)/x\u00b2. Wait, but is that the same as the original integrand?\n\nWait, sqrt(1 + x)/x^(5/2) = sqrt(1 + x)/ (x^2 * sqrt(x)) = sqrt(1 + x)/sqrt(x) * 1/x^2 = sqrt((1 + x)/x) * 1/x\u00b2 = sqrt(1 + 1/x) * 1/x\u00b2. Yes, exactly. So that substitution correctly transformed the integrand. Therefore, the integral is indeed - (2/3)(1 + 1/x)^(3/2) + C.\n\nAlternatively, the answer can be written in terms of x and (x + 1). Let me see:\n\n(1 + 1/x) = (x + 1)/x, so (1 + 1/x)^(3/2) = (x + 1)^(3/2)/x^(3/2). Therefore, the answer can be written as:\n\n- (2/3) * (x + 1)^(3/2)/x^(3/2) + C.\n\nWhich can also be written as - (2/3) * sqrt( (x + 1)^3 ) / x^(3/2 ) + C.\n\nAlternatively, sqrt((x +1)^3) = (x +1) s