stant, which implies that the image under \\( f \\cdot f \\cdot f \\) must be a single element of \\( A \\). Therefore, for any \\( x \\in A \\), \\( f(f(f(x))) \\) must be the same element for all \\( x \\in A \\).\n\nAccording to theorem (presumably referencing the problem):\n1. \\( f \\) is a bijection (since it must cycle through elements within \\( A \\)).\n2. Each cycle within \\( f \\) must be composed of either 1 element (a fixed point) or 3 elements (a 3-cycle). \n\nTherefore, \\( f \\) only has three possible configurations:\n1. \\( f \\) as a constant function.\n2. \\( f \\) with one 3-cycle and three fixed points.\n3. \\( f \\) with two 3-cycles.\n\nLet's examine each scenario in detail:\n\n#### Scenario 1: \\( f \\) as a constant function\n- This is impossible since \\( f \\cdot f \\cdot f \\) being constant \\( \\implies f \\) is bijective, and a constant function is not bijective.\n\n#### Scenario 2: \\( f \\) with one 3-cycle and three fixed points\n- We need to select 3 elements out of 6 to form the 3-cycle. This can be done in \\( \\binom{6}{3} \\) ways.\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{3} = \\frac{6!}{3!(6-3)!} = 20\n\\]\n\n- There are \\( 2! \\) (since it is a cycle of length 3, there are 2 non-unique permutations) ways to permute these 3 elements in a fixed cycle.\n\n\\[\n3! = 6 \\quad \\text{and thus} \\quad (3 - 1)! = 2!\n\\]\n\n- So the total number of functions \\( f \\) of this type is:\n\n\\[\n20 \\cdot 2 = 40\n\\]\n\n#### Scenario 3: \\( f \\) with two 3-cycles\n- We first choose 3 out of 6 elements for the first cycle and arrange, and then the remaining 3 automatically form the second cycle.\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\binom{6}{3}\n\\]\n\nThe factor \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) comes in because counting the same cycle configuration twice (one due to symmetry in pairing cycles). \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 20 = 10\n\\]\n\n- There are \\( 2! \\) ways to arrange each 3-cycle.\n\n\\[\n(3 - 1)! = 2\n\\]\n\n- Hence the total number of functions \\( f \\) for this 