e constraint setting and continual weakening reducing:\n   \n   $\\Rightarrow$ $\\boxed{1}$ (ultimate zero when unsuccessful hits dominant over indefinite.)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let $ABC$ be a triangle. Let $H_A$, $H_B$, and $H_C$ be the feet of the altitudes from $A$, $B$, and $C$, respectively. Show that $(AH_A)$ is the bisector of $\\overline{H_C H_A H_B}$.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to show that the altitude from A, which is AH_A, is the angle bisector of the angle formed at H_A by the points H_C, H_A, and H_B. Hmm, let me try to visualize this triangle. Let me draw triangle ABC with all three altitudes. The feet of the altitudes are H_A, H_B, and H_C. So H_A is the foot from A to BC, H_B is the foot from B to AC, and H_C is the foot from C to AB.\n\nNow, the problem says that AH_A is the bisector of the angle \u2220H_C H_A H_B. Wait, so we're looking at the angle at H_A between the points H_C and H_B. So angle H_C H_A H_B is the angle formed at H_A by connecting H_C to H_A and H_A to H_B. And the altitude AH_A should bisect this angle. That is, the line AH_A should split this angle into two equal parts.\n\nFirst, let me recall some properties of orthocentric systems or orthocenters. In a triangle, the orthocenter is the intersection point of the three altitudes. So, H_A, H_B, H_C are the feet of the altitudes, and their intersection is the orthocenter H. Wait, but in this problem, we are dealing with the feet of the altitudes, not the orthocenter itself. But maybe the orthocenter is involved in some way here.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I should consider cyclic quadrilaterals. In a triangle, the feet of the altitudes and the orthocenter form various cyclic quadrilaterals because of the right angles. For example, in triangle ABC, since AH_A is an altitude, then angle AH_A H_B is a right angle. Similarly, angle AH_A H_C is also a right angle. Wait, but no, AH_A is the alti