riginal triangle correspond to the angle bisectors of the orthic triangle.\n\nBut to make this rigorous, I need to provide a proof of this known result or derive it from more fundamental principles.\n\nAnother approach: consider the reflection of the orthocenter over the sides. It is known that reflecting the orthocenter over a side of the triangle gives a point on the circumcircle. For example, reflecting H over BC gives a point on the circumcircle of ABC. Similarly, reflecting over AB or AC also gives points on the circumcircle.\n\nBut how does this relate to the angle bisector? Let me try another way.\n\nIn the orthic triangle H_A H_B H_C, the original triangle's orthocenter H is the incenter of the orthic triangle. Wait, is this true? Wait, the incenter is the intersection of the angle bisectors. If H is the incenter of the orthic triangle, then the angle bisectors of H_A H_B H_C would pass through H. But the original altitudes pass through H. Therefore, perhaps the altitudes of ABC correspond to the angle bisectors of the orthic triangle.\n\nYes, in fact, in the orthic triangle, the altitudes of the original triangle serve as the angle bisectors. This is because each altitude of the original triangle is perpendicular to a side and passes through the orthocenter, which in the orthic triangle acts as the incenter.\n\nTherefore, AH_A, being an altitude of ABC, is an angle bisector of the orthic triangle H_A H_B H_C, specifically bisecting the angle at H_A.\n\nHence, we can conclude that AH_A bisects \u2220H_C H_A H_B.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{AH_A \\text{ is the angle bisector of } \\angle H_C H_A H_B}", "deepseek_solution": "To show that \\( (AH_A) \\) is the bisector of \\( \\angle H_C H_A H_B \\) in triangle \\( ABC \\), we consider the properties of the orthic triangle \\( H_A H_B H_C \\) and the role of the orthocenter.\n\n1. **Orthic Triangle**: The orthic triangle \\( H_A H_B H_C \\) is formed by the feet of the altitudes of triangle \\( ABC \\). The al