   - When \\( n = 1 \\), \\(\\gcd(a_1, a_0) = \\gcd(1, 0) = 1\\).\n   - When \\( n = 2 \\), \\(\\gcd(a_2, a_1) = \\gcd(1, 1) = 1\\).\n\n5. Assume for induction that \\(\\gcd(a_k, a_{k-1}) = 1\\) for some \\( k \\geq 2 \\). We need to show that \\(\\gcd(a_{k+1}, a_k) = 1\\):\n   \\[\n   \\gcd(a_{k+1}, a_k) = \\gcd(a_k + a_{k-1}, a_k) = \\gcd(a_k, a_{k-1}) = 1.\n   \\]\n\n6. Therefore, by induction, \\(\\gcd(a_n, a_{n-1}) = 1\\) holds for all \\( n \\geq 1 \\).\n\n7. Now, given any \\( m \\geq n \\), and using the Fibonacci recurrence relation and the substitution \\( m = qn + r \\) where \\( 0 \\leq r < n \\), we get:\n   \\[\n   \\gcd(a_m, a_n) = \\gcd(a_{qn + r}, a_n) = \\gcd(a_r, a_n).\n   \\]\n\n8. Here, the Euclidean algorithm tells us:\n   \\[\n   \\gcd(m, n) = \\gcd(r, n).\n   \\]\n\n9. Continuing the Euclidean algorithm process, this simplifies further to:\n   \\[\n   \\gcd(a_m, a_n) = a_{\\gcd(m, n)}.\n   \\]\n\n10. Substituting \\( m = 1988 \\) and \\( n = 1960 \\):\n   \\[\n   \\gcd(1988, 1960) = 28.\n   \\]\n\n11. Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\gcd(a_{1988}, a_{1960}) = a_{28}.\n   \\]\n\n12. Calculating \\( a_{28} \\) from the Fibonacci sequence:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{ll}\n   a_0 & = 0 \\\\\n   a_1 & = 1 \\\\\n   a_2 & = 1 \\\\\n   a_3 & = 2 \\\\\n   a_4 & = 3 \\\\\n   a_5 & = 5 \\\\\n   a_6 & = 8 \\\\\n   a_7 & = 13 \\\\\n   a_8 & = 21 \\\\\n   a_9 & = 34 \\\\\n   a_{10} & = 55 \\\\\n   a_{11} & = 89 \\\\\n   a_{12} & = 144 \\\\\n   a_{13} & = 233 \\\\\n   a_{14} & = 377 \\\\\n   a_{15} & = 610 \\\\\n   a_{16} & = 987 \\\\\n   a_{17} & = 1597 \\\\\n   a_{18} & = 2584 \\\\\n   a_{19} & = 4181 \\\\\n   a_{20} & = 6765 \\\\\n   a_{21} & = 10946 \\\\\n   a_{22} & = 17711 \\\\\n   a_{23} & = 28657 \\\\\n   a_{24} & = 46368 \\\\\n   a_{25} & = 75025 \\\\\n   a_{26} & = 121393 \\\\\n   a_{27} & = 196418 \\\\\n   a_{28} & = 317811 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   So the number we are looking for is:\n   \\[\n   a_{28} = 317811.\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n