nr_0 = \\frac{3V}{S_1 + S_2 + S_3 - S_0}.\n\\]\nRearranging gives:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} r_0 (S_1 + S_2 + S_3 - S_0).\n\\]\n\n**Conclusion**\nThe formula arises from extending the 2D exradius concept to 3D, where the exradius scales with the volume and the net effective area of the faces adjacent to the exsphere.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\[\n\\boxed{V = \\frac{1}{3}(S_1 + S_2 + S_3 - S_0) \\cdot r_0}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "## Solution:\n\n1. Let point \\(O\\) be the center of a sphere with radius \\(r_0\\), which touches the face \\(ABC\\) and the extensions of the faces \\(ABD\\), \\(BDC\\), and \\(ADC\\) of the tetrahedron \\(ABCD\\). \n\nDefine the areas of these faces as follows:\n\\[\nS_{\\triangle ABC} = S_0, \\quad S_{\\triangle ADB} = S_1, \\quad S_{\\triangle BDC} = S_2, \\quad S_{\\triangle ADC} = S_3\n\\]\n\n2. Connect point \\(O\\) with all vertices of the tetrahedron \\(A, B, C,\\) and \\(D\\). This results in dividing the original tetrahedron into four smaller triangular pyramids: \\(OABC\\), \\(OABD\\), \\(OBDC\\), and \\(OADC\\).\n\n3. Consider each of these pyramids separately. Given their common apex \\(O\\) and each having height \\(r_0\\) with respect to the respective base area, the volume calculation for each pyramid can be derived using the core volume formula for pyramids:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\text{Base Area} \\times \\text{Height}\n\\]\n\n4. Compute the volumes of these four pyramids individually:\n    - For pyramid \\(OABD\\):\n    \\[\n    V_{OABD} = \\frac{1}{3} S_{\\triangle ADB} \\cdot r_0 = \\frac{1}{3} S_1 \\cdot r_0\n    \\]\n    \n    - For pyramid \\(OBDC\\):\n    \\[\n    V_{OBDC} = \\frac{1}{3} S_{\\triangle BDC} \\cdot r_0 = \\frac{1}{3} S_2 \\cdot r_0\n    \\]\n    \n    - For pyramid \\(OADC\\):\n    \\[\n    V_{OADC} = \\frac{1}{3} S_{\\triangle ADC} \\cdot r_0 = \\frac{1}{3} S_3 \\cdot r_0\n    \\]\n    \n    - The original pyramid \\(OABC\\) (which was subtracted from the overall volume):\n    \\[\n    V_{OABC} = \\frac{1}{3} S_{\\triangle ABC} \\cdot r_0 = \\frac{1}{3} S_0 \\cdot r_0\n    \\]\n\n5. The total volume \\(V\\) of the tetrahedron \\(ABCD\\) can be computed by summing the volumes of the smaller pyramids and subtracting the volume of \\(OABC\\):\n\\[\nV = V_{OABD} + V_{OBDC} + V_{OADC} - V_{OABC}\n\\]\n\n6. Substitute the computed volumes:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} S_1 \\cdot r_0 + \\frac{1}{3} S_2 \\cdot r_0 + \\frac{1}{3} S_3 \\cdot r_0 - \\frac{1}{3} S_0 \\cdot r_0\n\\]\n\n7. Factor out the common term \\(\\frac{1}{3} r_0\\):\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} r_0 (S_1 + S_2 + S_3 - S_0)\n\\]\n\nThus, the formula for the volume of the tetrahedron is derived as:\n\\[\n\\boxed{V = \\frac{1}{3} (S_1 + S_2 + S_3 - S_0) \\cdot r_0}\n\\]\n\nThis concludes the proof. $\\blacksquare$", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find the volume of a regular quadrangular prism if the angle between the prism's diagonal and a side face is \\(\\alpha\\), and the side of the base is \\(a\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the volume of a regular quadrangular prism. The problem states that the angle between the prism's diagonal and a side face is \u03b1, and the side of the base is a. Hmm, let's start by recalling what a regular quadrangular prism is. A regular quadrangular prism has a base that's a square, right? So the base is a square with side length a, and the height of the prism, which I'll call h, is perpendicular to the base. The volume of a prism is the area of the base times the height, so in this case, the volume should be a\u00b2 * h. That part seems straightforward. The challenge here is figuring out the height h in terms of the given angle \u03b1 and the side length a.\n\nNow, the problem mentions the angle between the prism's diagonal and a side face is \u03b1. Let me visualize this. A prism has several diagonals. There are face diagonals and the space diagonal. But since the problem refers to the pri