pairs\n\nTotal pairs: \\( 9 + 70 + 20 = 99 \\).\n\nThus, the number of valid pairs \\( \\langle m, n \\rangle \\) is \\(\\boxed{99}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "## Problem:\n\n$S(n)$ \u8868\u793a\u81ea\u7136\u6570 $n$ \u7684\u6570\u5b57\u548c, \u6bd4\u5982 $S(123)=1+2+3=6$ \u3002\u5982\u679c\u4e24\u4e2a\u4e0d\u540c\u7684\u6b63\u6574\u6570 $m \u3001 n$, \u6ee1\u8db3\n$$\n\\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{c}\nm < 100 \\\\\nn < 100 \\\\\nm > n \\\\\nm + S(n) = n + 2S(m)\n\\end{array}\n\\right.\n$$\n\u6211\u4eec\u79f0 $m\u3001n$ \u6784\u6210\u4e00\u4e2a\u6570\u5bf9 $\\langle m, n \\rangle$\u3002\n\n\u8bf7\u95ee\u4e00\u5171\u6709\u591a\u5c11\u5bf9\u8fd9\u6837\u7684\u6570\u5bf9\uff1f\n\n## Solution:\n\nGiven the condition that $m + S(n) = n + 2S(m)$, we start by analyzing the properties of $S(n)$, which is the sum of the digits of $n$.\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution:\n\n1. **Summation Property Modulo 9:**\n   We know that for any integer $n$, $n \\equiv S(n) \\pmod{9}$. This is because the sum of the digits of $n$ is congruent to $n$ modulo 9. Thus:\n   $$\n   n \\equiv S(n) \\pmod{9}\n   $$\n\n2. **Simplification and Re-plugging:**\n   Now, using the given equation $m + S(n) = n + 2S(m)$:\n   $$\n   m + S(n) = n + 2S(m)\n   $$\n   Rearranging the terms:\n   $$\n   m - n = 2S(m) - S(n)\n   $$\n\n3. **Modulo 9 Analysis:**\n   Since $n - S(n) \\equiv 0 \\pmod{9}$, we can write:\n   $$\n   n - S(n) \\equiv m - 2S(m) \\pmod{9}\n   $$\n   Thus:\n   $$\n   S(m) \\equiv (n - S(n)) - (m - 2S(m)) \\pmod{9}\n   $$\n   Simplifying:\n   $$\n   0 \\equiv (n - S(n)) - (m - S(m)) \\pmod{9}\n   $$\n\n4. **Properties of $m$:**\n   Since $m \\equiv 0 \\pmod{9}$ from our earlier steps, $m$ must be a multiple of 9 and less than 100:\n   $$\n   m = 9, 18, 27, 36, 45, 54, 63, 72, 81, 90, 99\n   $$\n\n5. **Calculate Corresponding $n$:**\n   We use:\n   $$\n   n - S(n) = m - 2S(m)\n   $$\n   Let $n = \\overline{ab} = 10a + b$ where $a$, $b$ are digits. Then $S(n) = a + b$:\n   $$\n   n - S(n) = 9a\n   