by verifying distances \\( CQ \\) and \\( QP \\) in specific and general cases.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThrough systematic coordinate analysis and verification, it is demonstrated that \\( Q \\) is the midpoint of \\( CP \\), hence proving \\( CQ = QP \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{CQ = QP}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. We assume that \\(CA > CB\\).\n\n2. Notice that \\(H'\\) and \\(I'\\) are reflections of \\(H\\) and \\(I\\) across points \\(F\\) and \\(G\\) respectively. Hence, \\(H'\\) lies on the line segment \\(CF\\), and \\(I'\\) lies on the line segment \\(CG\\).\n\n3. Since \\(D\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\) and from the power of a point theorem applied to point \\(C\\) with respect to the circle \\(\\Gamma\\):\n\n\\[\nCH' \\cdot CA = AH \\cdot AC = AD^2 = BD^2 = BI \\cdot BC = CI' \\cdot CB\n\\]\n\n   This implies:\n   \n\\[\nCH' \\cdot CF = CI' \\cdot CG\n\\]\n\n4. Thus, \\(H', I', G, F\\) are concyclic.\n\n5. Since \\(H', I', G, F\\) are concyclic, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\angle I' H' C = \\angle CGF\n\\]\n\n6. Let \\(R, S\\) be the second intersections of \\(DF\\) and \\(DG\\) with the circle \\(\\Gamma\\), respectively.\n\n7. We want to show that \\(R\\) and \\(S\\) lie on the line \\(H'I'\\).\n\n8. From the power of a point theorem applied to \\(F\\) with respect to the circle that passes through \\(A, D, H', R\\):\n\n\\[\nFH' \\cdot FA = FH \\cdot FC = FR \\cdot FD\n\\]\n\n   Therefore, \\(A, D, H', R\\) are concyclic.\n   \n9. Consequently, \n\n\\[\n\\angle RH'F = \\angle FDA = \\angle CGF = \\angle I'H'C\n\\]\n\n   Hence, \\(R, H', I'\\) are collinear.\n   \n10. Similarly, \\(S, H', I'\\) are also collinear.\n\n11. Therefore, \\(R, H', Q, I', S, M\\) are concyclic.\n\n12. Observing that \\(M\\) lies on both \\(H'I'\\) and \\(FG\\):\n\n\\[\nMH' \\cdot MI' = MF \\cdot MG = MR \\cdot MS = MP \\cdot MC\n\\]\n\n   This implies \\(C, P, I', H'\\) are concyclic, and let this circle be \\(\\Gamma'\\).\n\n13. Given the cyclic quadrilateral configurations, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\angle H'CQ = \\angle SDC = \\angle SRC\n\\]\n\n   and\n\n\\[\n\\angle QCI' = \\angle CDR = \\angle CSR\n\\]\n\n   Hence, \\( \\triangle CH'Q \\sim \\triangle RCQ \\) and \\( \\triangle CI'Q \\sim \\triangle SCQ \\).\n\n14. Due to the similarity, we get:\n\n\\[\nQH' \\cdot QR = QC^2 = QI' \\cdot QS\n\\]\n\n15. By inverting with respect to point \\(Q\\) with power \\(QC\\) and radius \\(QC/2\\), the points \\(R, C, S\\) transform to \\(H', C, I'\\) respectively. Thus, the circumcircle \\(\\Gamma\\) of \\(\\triangle RCS\\) maps to the circumcircle \\(\\Gamma'\\) of \\(\\triangle H'CI'\\).\n\n16. Since point \\(P\\) lies on both these circles and inverting \\(C\\) about \\(Q\\) does not change \\(C\\), point \\(P\\) remains \\(P\\), satisfying \\(QP^2 = QC^2\\), hence \\(QP = QC\\).\n\n### Conclusion\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{C Q=Q P}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given a parallelogram \\(ABCD\\), the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of triangle \\(ABC\\) intersects side \\(AD\\) and the extension of side \\(DC\\) at points \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) respectively for the second time. Prove that the center of the circumcircle of triangle \\(PDQ\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that the center of the circumcircle of triangle PDQ lies on the circumcircle \u03c9 of triangle ABC in a parallelogram ABCD. Let me start by understanding the problem and visualizing the figure.\n\nFirst, let's recall that in a parallelogram ABCD, opposite sides are equal and parallel. So, AB is parallel and equal to CD, and AD is parallel and equal to BC. Also, diagonals bisect each other. Now, the circumcircle \u03c9 of triangle ABC. Since ABCD is a parallelogram, points A, B, C, D are arranged such that if I draw ABCD, AB and DC are the bases, and AD and BC are the sides.\n\nNow, the circumcircle \u03c9 of triangle ABC intersects side AD again at point P and the extension of side DC again at po