ed. So, we're starting with the sequence 1, 2, 3, ..., 2017 and want to end up with 2, 3, ..., 2017, 1. The transformation required here is a cyclic permutation where each element is shifted one position to the left, and the first element moves to the end. That is, 1 goes to position 2017, and every other number n goes to position n-1.\n\nNow, Yang is using 2016 swaps to achieve this permutation. Each swap is a transposition, which in permutation terms is a 2-cycle. The question is about counting the number of different sequences of swaps (i.e., the number of distinct sequences of transpositions) that result in this specific cyclic permutation.\n\nFirst, let me recall some permutation group theory. A cyclic permutation of length k can be written as a product of transpositions. The number of transpositions needed to decompose a cycle of length k is k-1. Here, we have a cycle of length 2017 (since we're moving each element one position left, which is a single cycle), so the minimal number of transpositions required is 2017 - 1 = 2016. That's exactly the number of swaps Yang is making. Therefore, each sequence of swaps corresponds to a decomposition of the cycle (1 2 3 ... 2017) into 2016 transpositions.\n\nHowever, the problem isn't just about the number of decompositions, but the number of sequences of swaps. Since swaps are performed in order, the sequence matters. Therefore, the problem is asking for the number of minimal-length factorizations of the cyclic permutation into transpositions, considering the order of multiplication (since applying swaps in a different order can result in different intermediate steps even if the final permutation is the same).\n\nBut permutation multiplication is done from right to left, so if we have a sequence of swaps s_1, s_2, ..., s_{2016}, the resulting permutation is s_{2016} ... s_2 s_1. So we need the product of these transpositions to equal the cycle (1 2 3 ... 2017). The number of such sequences is the number of minimal fact