0b2 + c x - c\u00b2 = 0, so coefficients are a=1, b=c, c=-c\u00b2.\n\nWait, discriminant is b\u00b2 - 4ac = c\u00b2 - 4*1*(-c\u00b2) = c\u00b2 + 4 c\u00b2 = 5 c\u00b2.\n\nTherefore, x = [-c \u00b1 sqrt(5 c\u00b2)] / 2 = [-c \u00b1 c sqrt(5)] / 2.\n\nSince x must be positive (as it's a length), we discard the negative solution:\n\nx = [ -c + c sqrt(5) ] / 2 = c ( -1 + sqrt(5) ) / 2.\n\nSo, DB = x = c ( sqrt(5) - 1 ) / 2.\n\nThen, AD = c - x = c - c ( sqrt(5) - 1 ) / 2 = c [ 1 - (sqrt(5) - 1)/2 ] = c [ (2 - sqrt(5) + 1 ) / 2 ] = c [ (3 - sqrt(5)) / 2 ].\n\nBut since AD is supposed to be the smaller segment, let's check if AD < DB.\n\nAD = c (3 - sqrt(5))/2 \u2248 c (3 - 2.236)/2 \u2248 c (0.764)/2 \u2248 0.382 c.\n\nDB = c (sqrt(5) -1)/2 \u2248 c (2.236 -1)/2 \u2248 c (1.236)/2 \u2248 0.618 c.\n\nYes, 0.382 < 0.618, so AD is indeed the smaller segment. Good.\n\nNow, in a right triangle, the altitude to the hypotenuse relates the segments AD and DB. There's a geometric mean theorem which states that the length of the altitude is the geometric mean of the lengths of the two segments. So, CD = sqrt(AD * DB).\n\nLet me compute CD:\n\nCD = sqrt( AD * DB ) = sqrt( [c (3 - sqrt(5))/2 ] * [c (sqrt(5) -1)/2 ] )\n\nMultiply inside the square root:\n\nFirst, multiply the constants:\n\n(3 - sqrt(5))(sqrt(5) -1) = 3 sqrt(5) -3 - sqrt(5)*sqrt(5) + sqrt(5) = 3 sqrt(5) -3 -5 + sqrt(5) = (3 sqrt(5) + sqrt(5)) -8 = 4 sqrt(5) -8.\n\nTherefore:\n\nCD = sqrt( c\u00b2 (4 sqrt(5) -8 ) / 4 ) = sqrt( c\u00b2 (sqrt(5) -2 ) ) = c sqrt( sqrt(5) -2 ).\n\nWait, sqrt(5) is approximately 2.236, so sqrt(5) -2 \u2248 0.236. So sqrt( sqrt(5) -2 ) \u2248 sqrt(0.236) \u2248 0.486. But this seems a bit messy. Maybe there's another way to approach this.\n\nAlternatively, since CD is the altitude, and the area of the triangle is (base * height)/2. The hypotenuse is c, so area is also (AB * CD)/2 = (c * CD)/2.\n\nBut the area can also be calculated as (leg1 * leg2)/2. Maybe we can find the legs in terms of c